WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:04.890
As your applications grow in complexity
how do you harness and drive new

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:10.019
insights from all the chaos? And is
observability just a buzzword, or is it

00:00:10.019 --> 00:00:14.400
something that you actually need to
think about? Spoiler alert, it is. My name

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.940
is Sai Vennam and I'm with the IBM Cloud
team, and today I'm joined with a special

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:22.560
guest. Hi there, I'm Laura Santamaría and
I am a Developer Advocate with LogDNA.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.539
If you don't know LogDNA is a core part
of our observability story on IBM Cloud,

00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:31.380
but today we're gonna be talking about
observability, so let's start with

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:36.210
definition. So observability is a
property of your systems that helps you

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:40.559
understand what's going on with them,
monitor what they're doing, and be able

00:00:40.559 --> 00:00:44.129
to get the information you need to
troubleshoot. So the way we see it

00:00:44.129 --> 00:00:47.670
there's three major tears of
observability and let's go through those now.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:53.250
We're gonna start out with my favorite
which is logging. In addition to logging

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:58.559
we additionally have metrics, so that's
just all of your analytics around all of

00:00:58.559 --> 00:01:02.340
the data that you're gathering and
finally...we've got monitoring. Now

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:05.760
monitoring is essentially putting up a
magnifying glass to your systems and

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.720
getting new insights from what's
actually running there. Today we're gonna

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.059
be starting with an example,

00:01:11.059 --> 00:01:13.260
in the
bottom left corner we have sketched out

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:16.890
a few of the different infrastructure
pieces so we'll start with today. Can we

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:21.600
explain what those are? Sure, we have a
public cloud, it can be any of them. And then

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.159
you have on-prem, and then let's say we
actually have some user data, maybe this

00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:31.530
is a tablet or a cell phone. So all of
those infrastructure pieces are creating

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:36.150
and generating data and what I'm kind of
gonna focus on here is the personas that

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:43.759
are going to consume them. So we've got
that Dev persona,

00:01:43.759 --> 00:01:47.299
we've got Ops,

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:57.510
and finally we have Security. So, all of
this data flowing in is kind of a lot, I

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:02.550
want to have some way of filtering it
down for my specific user personas to be

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:06.990
able to understand it. So let's start
with developers,

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:11.280
what do developers care about? I actually
want to back up here for a moment though

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:14.400
because let's talk about all the
different levels that logging can come

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.280
from. So we have three different levels
that we can think about so you have your

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:25.110
Operating System, you have Kubernetes or
any other type of platform, so I'm

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:31.290
picking kubernetes. That's my favorite. And
then finally your application. So your

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:35.700
operating system and kubernetes all send
really good logs and you can use a lot

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:41.610
of that data pretty much as this, or add
in some of your own but applications is

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:47.010
really where you need to spend some time.
So you're devs need to create a proper

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:52.380
event stream and this really goes by the
garbage in, garbage out system where you

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:56.160
really need to put in good work and get
some good data on the side of the

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.989
application so that you get good logs
out. Right, exactly, so the great

00:03:00.989 --> 00:03:04.200
developers out there on kubernetes and
the operating Systems they've

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:07.560
instrumented their platforms but the
application that's up to you as a

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.160
developer to make sure the
instrumentation is in place. Absolutely,

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:17.430
and when you think about it, let's say
that we have an operating system here

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:20.400
and I'm gonna say that's an operating
system, and then we have kubernetes

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:28.820
running on it. And then you actually have
your app running on top of kubernetes.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:36.209
And all of these are to each sending
data. So we have three different levels

00:03:36.209 --> 00:03:41.130
of data all coming out and trying to
come towards the dev that wants some

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:44.910
information. Right, so it looks like
they're all coming into this central

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:49.380
area here. That's right. We can talk about
this is our aggregator.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:58.680
So our aggregator takes in all of this
data and puts it all into one place so

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:01.240
we can work with it. That's right, but
kind of

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:05.140
coming back to the the problem here a
developer might not care about all of

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:09.520
the information flowing in, how do we
drive just the pieces that they care

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:12.880
about like we mentioned? Maybe they
instrumented their specific application,

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:19.600
how do we drive that to them? Absolutely,
so an aggregator often has filters. So in

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:26.110
this case let's say the dev is just
asking for data about debugging and just

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:30.550
some information there, and your data,
your filter can actually set up a

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:35.500
dashboard or some other way of accessing
all of that data that the dev can take a

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:40.090
look at just the pieces that they need.
That's a core part of a observability

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:43.990
solution, this aggregator not only does
it collect the data but it needs to

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:48.970
externalize it, expose it, so my
developers can access it and drive new

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:52.480
insights. So let's say we solved that
part of the puzzle,

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:55.960
what do operators care about? What are
the operations teams? What are they

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:59.920
looking for out of these systems? So an
operations team might need to know more

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:04.240
about degradation of its system, or if a
pod is falling over,  maybe your database

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:07.780
filled up and you need to know more
information about how you can fix it.

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:11.860
The ops teams is going to be getting
data from all of these different systems

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:18.160
and filtering it out to yet another
dashboard or another interface of some

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:24.010
sort and getting that data just what
they need. Right, so potentially they may

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:28.540
not care as much about specific
application level logs but they'll be

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:32.560
looking to kubernetes to say hey what
was the CPU usage, do we need to set up

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:37.290
some horizontal pod auto scalers to make
sure that we don't hit those limits.

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:41.230
Finally, kind of probably see where I'm
going here with the last piece of the

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:45.040
puzzle with security, they probably have
a dashboard that's created for them as

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:49.270
well. So a security team let's say
they're using a third-party tool as most

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:54.190
security teams generally, do they
identify a threat ID, or maybe a customer

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:58.480
ID and they want to dive in deeper to a
potential threat that's been identified.

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:02.230
So they put that information in the
aggregator and they can identify and

00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:06.700
make kind of sense of all the chaos to
identify exactly what that specific

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:10.840
security analyst might be looking for.
But I want to pose an interesting

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:13.990
question here, it's not always about
going to the system and identifying

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:17.860
what's there,
many times security advisors need to

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:21.580
know what's happening the second it
happens and they can't just sit there

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:25.239
and stare at logs all day, right.
Absolutely, this is where monitoring

00:06:25.239 --> 00:06:30.849
comes in, this is really a two-way street.
We have automated alerts that can go out

00:06:30.849 --> 00:06:35.229
and tell all of these different groups
about specific things that they're

00:06:35.229 --> 00:06:40.569
interested in, specific events that they
want to know about. So let's say that you

00:06:40.569 --> 00:06:44.589
have a system that's been accessed and
it's not supposed to be frankly that

00:06:44.589 --> 00:06:49.089
system is going to figure it out long
before a human is and that's what an

00:06:49.089 --> 00:06:53.110
alert is for an ops team doesn't want to
find out that there's a degradation of

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:58.209
service when their user does, they need
to know ahead of time. So a good

00:06:58.209 --> 00:07:02.319
observability solution should have the
ability to externalize the data and then

00:07:02.319 --> 00:07:06.939
additionally set up a learning on top of
that. So our dev team may be their most

00:07:06.939 --> 00:07:12.729
comfortable in Slack, so they set up a
chat bot so that particular exceptions

00:07:12.729 --> 00:07:16.869
when they're thrown they're able to know
when they happen. Your ops team may be

00:07:16.869 --> 00:07:20.889
they were using something like a paging
system so that you know in the middle of

00:07:20.889 --> 00:07:24.459
the night if something goes down they
get alert and they can start looking

00:07:24.459 --> 00:07:28.089
into it right away. And then finally for
our security teams, kind of as I

00:07:28.089 --> 00:07:31.569
mentioned, they're generally using you
know maybe third party tools or custom

00:07:31.569 --> 00:07:37.029
dashboards they can set up custom
alerting so they can know exactly when

00:07:37.029 --> 00:07:43.059
something goes down. And to be honest
this is your new norm, you're going to

00:07:43.059 --> 00:07:46.869
have multiple clouds, you're going to
have on-premise systems, you're going to

00:07:46.869 --> 00:07:51.999
have data coming directly in from your
users. You need to be able to understand

00:07:51.999 --> 00:07:57.279
what's going on and really this is what
observability is all about. Thanks for

00:07:57.279 --> 00:08:00.939
joining us for this quick overview of
observability, also thank you so much for

00:08:00.939 --> 00:08:04.449
joining us today Laura. Absolutely, my
pleasure. If you have any questions

00:08:04.449 --> 00:08:07.929
please drop us a line below. If you want
to see more videos like this in the

00:08:07.929 --> 00:08:12.219
future, please like and subscribe. And
